artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $D^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ denote the closed two-dimensional disk, equipped with a smooth area form whose induced probability measure is $\mu$. Let $\mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$ be the space of $C^1$ diffeomorphisms of $D^2$ that preserve $\mu$, endowed with the $C^1$ topology. For $f \in \mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$, define the maximal integrated Lyapunov exponent by
$$ \lambda^+(f,\mu) \;=\; \int_{D^2} \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{1}{n} \log \| Df^n_x \| \, d\mu(x), $$
and let $h_\mu(f)$ denote the metric (Kolmogorov–Sinai) entropy of $f$ with respect to $\mu$. Set
$$ \mathcal{G} \;=\; \{\, f \in \mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2) : \lambda^+(f,\mu) = 0 \,\}. $$
Decide which of the following two mutually exclusive alternatives holds:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $\mathcal{G}$ is a dense $G_\delta$ subset of $\mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$;
\item[(b)] there exist a nonempty $C^1$-open set $\mathcal{U} \subset \mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$ and a constant $\delta > 0$ such that $h_\mu(g) \geq \delta$ for every $g \in \mathcal{U}$.
\end{itemize}
